  "@context": {
    "@vocab": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#",
    "rdf": "http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#",
    "rdfs": "http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#",
    "owl": "http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#"
  },
  "@id": "StereotypedQua-entitiesKnowledgeBase",
  "@type": "owl:Class",
  "rdfs:subClassOf": "BackgroundKnowledge",
  "dimensionIndex": 14,
  "rdfs:comment": "Qua-entities are entities considered while satisfying a certain role, and in particular focusing on one specific feature or dimension that qualifies them as such. Qua-entities are often used to describe a certain group of people in a stereotypical way, e.g. 'black skin tone' when we see an image of a man, instead of focusing e.g. on the man's profession, age, etc. These elements as well could be used as qua-entities. For example 'a hijab' could be a qua-entity pointing at the religious marker when we see an image of a woman wearing a hijab. This knowledge base includes features that can be used as stereotypes often used to describe a certain group of people in a stereotypical way.",
  "rdfs:label": "Stereotyped Qua-entities Knowledge Base",
  "promptExtractionText": "Qua-entities are entities considered while satisfying a certain role, and in particular focusing on one specific feature or dimension that qualifies them as such. Qua-entities are often used to describe a certain group of people in a stereotypical way, e.g. 'black skin tone' when we see an image of a man, instead of focusing e.g. on the man's profession, age, etc. These elements as well could be used as qua-entities. For example 'a hijab' could be a qua-entity pointing at the religious marker when we see an image of a woman wearing a hijab. This knowledge base includes features that can be used as stereotypes often used to describe a certain group of people in a stereotypical way.\nSome of the possible features, among others could be:\nPHYSICAL APPEARANCE MARKERS\nSkin tone/complexion\nDarkness or lightness of skin\nSkin tone contrasts in group settings\nExaggerated rendering of skin color\nFacial features\nNose shape/size\nLip fullness\nEye shape (especially epicanthic folds)\nFacial hair patterns\nJawline/facial structure\nHair characteristics\nTexture (kinky, straight, wavy)\nStyle (dreadlocks, hijab, turban, kippah)\nColor\nHead coverings with religious/cultural significance\nBody type/physique\nWeight/body size\nMuscle definition\nHeight representations\nGendered body characteristics\n\nCLOTHING & ADORNMENT\nReligious markers\nHijab, niqab, burqa\nKippah, tzitzit\nTurbans (Sikh)\nCrucifixes, religious jewelry\nModest dress patterns\nCultural clothing\nTraditional garments (sari, kimono, dashiki)\nHead wraps/scarves\nSpecific footwear\nSocioeconomic signifiers\n\"Urban\" fashion (baggy clothes, specific brands)\nLuxury brand display\nWork uniforms\nWorn/damaged clothing\nJewelry abundance or type\nGendered presentation\nFeminine/masculine coded clothing\nGender non-conforming presentation\nMakeup presence/style\n\nBEHAVIORAL & GESTURAL CUES\nPhysical actions\nDancing styles\nPrayer positions\nHand gestures (especially culturally specific ones)\nEating methods\nPhysical labor vs. sedentary activity\nAthletic performance\nSpatial behavior\nCrowding/group size\nProximity to certain locations (borders, water, etc.)\nPublic vs. private space occupation\nInteraction patterns\nFamily groupings (especially number of children)\nGender segregation or mixing\nHierarchical arrangements\n\nCONTEXTUAL ENVIRONMENT\nLocation markers\nUrban settings (especially \"inner city\" imagery)\nRural/agricultural settings\nGeographic markers (deserts, jungles, specific countries)\nInstitutional settings (welfare offices, prisons)\nReligious spaces (mosques, synagogues)\nBorder/checkpoint imagery\nMaterial surroundings\nHousing quality\nVehicle types\nFood being consumed\nPresence of weapons\nTechnology usage or absence\nCleanliness/disorder of environment\n\nTEMPORAL & ACTIVITY MARKERS\nTime-of-day implications\nDaytime work vs. nighttime activity\nSeasonal contexts\nActivity type\nManual labor\nIntellectual work\nLeisure activities\nReligious practices\nConsumption patterns\n\nCOMPARATIVE & RELATIONAL FEATURES\nJuxtaposition markers\nBefore/after comparisons\nSide-by-side with animals\nProximity to objects/symbols\nScale relationships (making subjects appear larger/smaller)\nNumerical representation\nCrowd size emphasis\nIsolation vs. group representation\nRatio of in-group to out-group\n\nSYMBOLS & PROPS\nObjects in frame\nSpecific foods (watermelon, fried chicken, etc.)\nWeapons\nMoney/currency\nReligious texts or symbols\nFlags and national symbols\nAlcohol or drugs\nBackground elements\nGraffiti\nSignage in specific languages\nArchitectural styles\nNatural vs. built environments\n\nVISUAL TREATMENT & FRAMING\nImage manipulation\nExaggeration of features\nColor saturation/desaturation\nZooming on specific body parts\nCropping to emphasize certain elements\nFilters that alter appearance\nFraming choices\nCamera angle (looking up/down at subjects)\nDistance (intimate vs. distant)\nWhat's centered in frame\nWhat's visible vs. cropped out\n\nINTERSECTION EFFECTS\nMultiple marker combinations\nSkin tone + location + activity\nClothing + gesture + group size\nReligious marker + behavior + environment\nThe toxic potential increases exponentially when multiple markers align, as they create a more specific stereotype activation pattern.\n\nSYSTEM DESIGN CONSIDERATIONS\nFor your content moderation system, you'd want to:\nFeature detection pipelines that identify these visual elements\nText-image alignment scoring that measures semantic relationships\nContext sensitivity - same image + different text = different toxicity levels\nCultural knowledge bases - what's innocuous in one context is harmful in another\nCombination rules - certain visual features only become problematic with specific text overlays\n"
}
